# A compile-time child process dies with its parent: a `jet check`, `jet
# build` or `jet run` killed (SIGKILL included) while its compile-time work
# runs takes the process that work runs in with it. The child holds the
# read end of a pipe whose write end only jet holds, set to signal the
# child (SIGIO, whose default ends it) once that end closes, which the
# kernel does however jet ends. PR_SET_PDEATHSIG can't do this: it follows
# the thread that started the child, which may end first.
# left.sh starts the command as a shell does, kills it once its compile-time
# work runs, and counts the processes below it that outlive it.
requires: os-name:linux
fixture: Fixtures/ComptimeChildDiesWithItsParent
env: NO_COLOR=1
step: sh left.sh check
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "jet: 137\nleft: 0\n" # the check ends on the KILL, and nothing below it outlives it
stdout: !~ "below: 0\n" # the check's compile-time work runs in a child
step: sh left.sh build
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "jet: 137\nleft: 0\n" # the build ends on the KILL, and nothing below it outlives it
stdout: !~ "below: 0\n" # the build's compile-time work runs in a child
step: sh left.sh run
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "jet: 137\nleft: 0\n" # the run ends on the KILL, and nothing below it outlives it
stdout: !~ "below: 0\n" # the run's compile-time work runs in a child
